# LUCI messaging voice

Always-applied. Canonical source: **LUCI Messaging Guide** (`LUCI Systems Design System/ui_kits/review/messaging/messaging-guide.html` → Voice & tone). The portal-shipped copy lives in `ui_kits/internal-portal/customization/_brand/SKILL.md` → **Voice & tone** — keep the two in sync.

## Voice rules

- **Engine and verbs — not layers.** Never use **layer** as a noun for LUCI ("orchestration layer," "application layer," "one layer for…"). Describing layers in the **client's** accumulated stack is fine; naming LUCI a layer is not.
- **Leading choices:** **orchestration engine** (positioning, tagline) and **LUCI orchestrates…** (headlines, capability copy). Also fine when they fit: **runs, operates, integrates, consolidates, refines, platform, infrastructure**.
- **Watch repetition.** If **engine** or **orchestrates** appears more than once in a short passage (deck section, PDF page, email), rotate to a precise alternative. Same word three times reads like a crutch.
- **Always write "A/V"** — never "AV" or "A-V" — in body copy, headlines, labels, captions, alt text, and diagrams.
- **Institutions, not adjectives.** Describe what the platform does for the enterprise, never how the software feels. "Operate every endpoint from one interface" passes; "absurdly simple" does not.
- **Subtraction over addition.** Lead with what LUCI removes — variables, vendors, interfaces, refresh cycles — not with the capabilities it adds.
- **Declarative over promotional.** State facts. Avoid emotional verbs ("revolutionize," "transform," "empower"). If a claim needs an adverb to land, cut the modifier, then the sentence.
- **Discretion over display.** No client names in public materials. No percentage claims. Scale and complexity in general terms.
- **Standardization is the advantage.** Never apologize for a standard approach. Customization is what broke every A/V environment they've had before.
- **Lean, not padded.** Cut modifiers a claim leans on. Favor lean copy, but vary sentence length and structure for flow — don't force every sentence short. Narrative is welcome where it carries more weight.

## Approved vs retired

- **Use:** orchestration engine, platform, infrastructure · LUCI orchestrates / runs / operates / integrates / consolidates / refines · institutional, operational, embedded, accountable, continuous · LUCI FDE, the embedded team, the standard · "removes variables," "shorter list," "fewer moving parts."
- **Retire:** absurdly simple / easy to use / intuitive · revolutionize / transform / empower · best-in-class / game-changing · owner's rep (use LUCI FDE or embedded team) · **layer** when naming LUCI · percentage claims in brand copy · named clients in public materials.

## Tone & voice (live website — the way we talk about LUCI)

Match the tone and stance, not the homepage's sentence structures. Do not canonize short-declarative or em-dash patterns as rules.

- **Lead with the customer's problem; stage LUCI as the solution.** Don't open with LUCI — establish the problem and stakes first so the reader knows why LUCI matters before it appears. LUCI is the means, not the subject. (Primary in marketing/sales copy; use in SOW/proposal where it aids persuasion.)
- **Frame the problem as accumulation, silos, and complexity.** "They fail because of what they accumulate." · "Today, separate A/V technology lives in separate silos." · "Complexity isn't solved by a better interface."
- **Stakes are operational, not aspirational.** "not optional," "non-negotiable," "cannot afford failure."
- **The team is permanent and accountable.** "with a team that stays," "built in, not bolted on."
- **Vary sentence structure for flow.** Lean is favored, but narrative is welcome where it carries more weight; don't force every sentence short or default to contrast pairs.
- **Register by document:** marketing/sales/web copy may use 2nd person; SOW/MSA/proposal are 3rd person, factual, no marketing register.
- **Verbatim:** tagline "The Orchestration Engine for Enterprise Multimedia"; sub-tagline "One interface to control, automate, and execute the entire guest experience."; value-prop boilerplate (Messaging Guide) — do not paraphrase.
- **Key phrases (reference, not required templates):** "Complexity isn't solved by a better interface. It's solved by a shorter list of things to manage." · "built in, not bolted on" · "with a team that stays."

## Scope notes

- Applies to everything LUCI publishes — website, decks, proposals, sales documents, outreach, social, video, diagrams.
- For portal document customization, each per-template `SKILL.md` carries the same compact contract and points here.
- Voice patches re-apply after Word sync via `transform-messaging-html.mjs`.
